A||||17||alpha||GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero Team?||||1||
Z||000000||alpha||05-09-2001||01:11 PM||||For the past couple years Grassroots Motorsports (www.grassrootmotorsports.com) has sponsored a challenge where readers must build a car for a set amount of money (next year will be $2002) and then that car must compete against the other cars in a drag race, autocross and a concours.<P>Clearly there needs to be a Fiero team. I am seriously considering starting a team. If anyone wants to participate, sell parts dirt cheap or has any input regarding this please let me know.<P>Also for more information on the $2002 event visit the GRM webpage. For more information on the $2001 event buy this months issue of GRM.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||05-09-2001||01:25 PM||||I've been dreaming of doing that very same thing all year. I think a Fiero would have a very good chance except in the drag, too heavy. Some of those car are hitting into the 12s.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||05-09-2001||01:27 PM||||Damn, 12s for 2000 bucks?  They use NOS or something?<P>I think if you started with a base model Fiero then stripped it for weight you could probably get it around 2300 lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-09-2001||01:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Damn, 12s for 2000 bucks?  They use NOS or something?<P>I think if you started with a base model Fiero then stripped it for weight you could probably get it around 2300 lbs.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah maybe a bit more if you remove some of the reinforcements from the Deck, Hood and rear clip,<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000004||alpha||05-09-2001||01:46 PM||||Unless somehow a huge motorswap is deemed possible I don't see it possible to attain 12s in the quarter even with NOS and a stripped car.<P>Doing well in this is just a matter of buying a car with potential at a favorable price (ie Fiero), buying 1 or more parts cars, fully recouping your investment in the parts cars by selling parts, buying select go fast parts used (ie NOS), and blowing a ton of grunt labor into the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||onfire||05-09-2001||03:37 PM||||Buy a car with a bad engine or no engine. <BR>Put in a caddy 4.9.<BR>Strip everything that you don't need off.<BR>Some cheap 15 or 16 inch wheels new tires.<BR>Rear sway bar.<BR>Hmmmmmmm what else?||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||05-09-2001||03:48 PM||||yap, 12s.<P>The 12 second cars for under $2001 were: MG Midget, 1987 Dodge Omni, and a 1979 Ford Mustang.<P>They have tons of interesting entries, even an '88 Yugo, but its 1/4 time was 17.176. Really cool competition. If I had $2000 to blow, and a car I didn't care about thrashing and blowing up, this contest would be for me. I'll just read about it for now.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||05-09-2001||04:11 PM||||I thought the car in the 12's was a Dodge minivan with a 2.2ltr turbo. Those things are potent 2.2ltr turbo's (not the minivan).<P>Get a base coupe and 1900lbs is attainable. Run a 4.9ltr engine , easy to build this car on a budget...<P>84 Base coupe with 4.10 Axle<BR>Caddy 4.5 , 4.9ltr engine<P>Run 2 rear sway bars one going each way to stiffen the rear of the car up (did this with a z51 Corvette bar to heavy for stree use). Caprice police springs , (they will fit a fiero strut, I have some) inginuity is the key<P>Gut the car<P>Run 13" wheels from a cavalier , it gets the car down lower , the 13" wheels are cheap and you can run cheaper autocross compound tires... Plus shorter tires means a even lowert gear ratio.. Forget bigger 15"-16" wheels. they raise the center of gravity too much.<P>Just remember to make a 84 to 87 Fiero work get the rear of the car hard as you can and the front fairly soft.<P>Just need a solid southern car as a good base<P>JM<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||alpha||05-09-2001||06:19 PM||||The only problem I see with this caddy engine thing is the flywheel...unless their is a cheap way around that I don't see it as do-able. Is it possible to retain the caddy automatic transmission or something?||||reg||1
Z||000009||stimpy||05-09-2001||11:30 PM||||Doug Kinney has a beetchin 84 coupe,yellow with chromallusion flames and a seized duke for sale on his website. $600 obo! There's your starting point.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||05-09-2001||11:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by onfire:<BR><B>Buy a car with a bad engine or no engine. <BR>Put in a caddy 4.9.<BR>Strip everything that you don't need off.<BR>Some cheap 15 or 16 inch wheels new tires.<BR>Rear sway bar.<P>Hmmmmmmm what else?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Strip the spaceframe and attack it with a 2" hole saw. Can you say "swiss cheese"? I'm sure there's lot's of metal that would have no impact on chassis stiffness.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||onfire||05-10-2001||02:39 AM||||Thanks for the added info.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B><BR>Run 13" wheels from a cavalier , it gets the car down lower , the 13" wheels are cheap and you can run cheaper autocross compound tires... Plus shorter tires means a even lowert gear ratio.. Forget bigger 15"-16" wheels. they raise the center of gravity too much.<P>Just remember to make a 84 to 87 Fiero work get the rear of the car hard as you can and the front fairly soft.<P>Just need a solid southern car as a good base<P>JM<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||05-10-2001||02:12 PM||||Doug,<P>Too bad we are on opposite sides of the country.  I would be more than happy to help you out on a project car for GRM.  I just love that magazine/website.<P>I wouldn't rule out 2.3L turbo Fords and 2-2.2L turbo DC's though.  Also look into a bone yard turbo upgrade a-la Dennis LaGrua.  <P>The only problem would be the same that has been mentioned previously... the Fiero is a PIG in the weight department.  It would take some serious power to keep up with Guido in his turbo DC MG||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||05-10-2001||02:55 PM||||I've already got my Fiero for the $2002 GRM Challenge. It's a Blue '87 GT 5-speed that I got for $300 (& it runs). It was soaked in a local flood a few years ago and the owner bought it back from the insurance company and re-built a lot of it. New parts in most of the suspension etc. It seems his wife got tired of looking at it in the garage and made him get rid of it. Following the GRM Rules, I can sell parts off of it but only up to the price of the car. So, I've sold the NEW seats he had for $300 and I'll put in a set of old take-outs. The engine runs great and since I can't sell it I'm talking locally to a Fiero owner who may want to trade me for a used SBC engine he has sitting in his garage.<P>I know a guy who sells V-8 kits and I'm gonna buy his base kit and buy all the other parts I need at Swap Meets. For now, until I get the engine installed, I'm planning to use the suspension & chassis as is. Then if there is any money left, I'll work on the suspension.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000014||alpha||05-10-2001||03:00 PM||||Archie you are a god! With that car and a nitrous injected 350 you can win this thing!<P>I passed on a v8 Fiero with spun rod bearings for $500 last fall and I have been kicking myself since... It would have been perfect for this!||||reg||1
Z||000015||onfire||05-10-2001||04:20 PM||||Archie when you are done with your $2002 Fiero, I will give you $2500 for it.||||reg||1
